@packages/compiler/src/event-protocol.sigil import { SigilCompilationEventProtocol }

component SigilCompilationEventWriter {
  goal {
    Own deterministic production and delivery settlement for one compiler event run
    under SigilCompilationEventProtocol without owning host transport selection or
    operation settlement.
  }

  interface {
    CompilationEventWriterProtocol {
      openWriter(writableSink, ExpectedCompilationRun) validates the expectation,
      generates a cryptographically random canonical lowercase UUID version 4 runId,
      and atomically attempts sequence-1 started. It returns ready with exactly that
      runId and one stateful writer only after full delivery; zero-byte failure
      returns stream-unavailable and partial delivery returns stream-incompatible,
      with no writer or started run.

      Invalid ExpectedCompilationRun returns protocol-invalid before runId generation
      or sink access and returns no writer.

      The writer exposes stageStarted(stageIdentity),
      diagnostic(CompilationDiagnostic, componentName?),
      stageCompleted(CompilationStageReport), completed(CompilationReport),
      failed(rejectionCode, message), and cancelled(message). It validates semantic
      values and constructs every schema field, JSON encoding, and frame.

      WritableEnvelopeSink writes one complete envelope per asynchronous call and
      truthfully settles with exactly delivered-all, rejected-zero-compatible,
      rejected-zero-unavailable, or rejected-partial. A generic throw, rejection, or
      undeclared result is conservatively stream-incompatible.

      The host sink owns zero-byte classification. rejected-zero-compatible means it
      observed that no byte escaped and the same binding remains able to accept one
      later terminal envelope. rejected-zero-unavailable means it observed that no
      byte escaped but the binding cannot accept a later call. If either fact cannot
      be established, the sink must not claim a zero-byte result; a generic or
      uncertain failure is treated as stream-incompatible.

      The protocol imposes no writer-side deadline. A sink call that never settles
      leaves its writer call and every accepted call behind it pending, commits no
      sequence, and changes no writer state. A host requiring bounded settlement
      supplies a sink that observes its deadline or cancellation, determines bytes
      delivered, and settles with the corresponding declared sink result.

      Calls serialize in synchronous call-acceptance order. The first accepted
      terminal closes acceptance immediately; any concurrently or subsequently
      submitted call returns protocol-invalid without writing after earlier accepted
      calls settle.

      Validate every call before consulting suppression. Invalid input or state
      returns protocol-invalid without writing and permanently closes the writer.
      Valid nonterminal calls return delivered, suppressed, stream-unavailable,
      stream-incompatible, or protocol-exhausted. Valid terminal calls return
      delivered, terminal-unavailable, stream-incompatible, or protocol-exhausted.

      If an accepted queued call reaches the head after an earlier call closed the
      writer, it performs no validation or write and uses the retained cause:
      protocol-invalid after invalid input, protocol-exhausted after exhaustion,
      stream-incompatible after partial delivery or undeclared sink failure, and
      stream-unavailable for a nonterminal or terminal-unavailable for a terminal
      after zero-byte unavailability. Every queued call uses the same retained cause.

      completed requires report runId, ordered stage identities, operation, and
      conditional session identity to equal the bound expectation. One-shot reports
      have no session. Any mismatch is protocol-invalid.
    }
  }
}

expand SigilCompilationEventWriter {
  constraints {
    CompilationEventWriterProtocol {
      Delegate envelope vocabulary and cross-direction ordering invariants to
      SigilCompilationEventProtocol.

      Sequence is committed only after full atomic delivery. Suppressed progress
      consumes no sequence. Requiring 4294967296 returns protocol-exhausted without
      writing and permanently closes the writer.

      A zero-byte compatible nonterminal failure returns suppressed and suppresses
      every later valid nonterminal after validation; exactly one terminal may still
      be attempted with the next delivered sequence. A zero-byte unavailable
      nonterminal failure returns stream-unavailable and closes the writer.

      Terminal zero-byte compatible or unavailable failure returns
      terminal-unavailable. Any partial envelope or generic sink failure returns
      stream-incompatible, closes the logical writer, marks the sink unusable for
      protocol writes, and forbids recovery on another stream. The host exclusively
      owns physical cleanup.

      A completed terminal requires every delivered stage-completed report to equal
      its terminal-report stage entry. Every delivered diagnostic must equal exactly
      one terminal-report currentDiagnostics entry by complete equality and
      fingerprint, and each stage diagnosticCount equals the report count for that
      stage. The report may include additional progress suppressed diagnostics.

      For completed reconciliation, an expected stage omitted before a later
      delivered interval must be disabled or skipped-by-dependency. An entirely
      suppressed suffix may be completed, failed, disabled, or skipped-by-dependency.
      Pending, running, or cancelled is invalid. An observably open stage must end as
      completed or failed. Failed and cancelled terminals require only the already
      validated, fingerprint-unique progress prefix because they carry no report.

      Own event production and delivery outcomes, but leave compilation, evaluation,
      session, persistence, cancellation policy, and method settlement to callers.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilationEventWriterProtocol {
      Decision: Use one single-attempt stateful writer with atomic progress
      suppression, irreversible partial-delivery failure, and explicit queued-call
      closure propagation.

      Trade-offs: A compatible lost progress event suppresses the remaining progress
      suffix; a partial write prevents recovery and terminal delivery.

      Consequences: Concurrent callers observe deterministic settlements and valid
      results never fail merely because retained command output is large.

      Discarded alternatives: Automatic retry was rejected because uncertain delivery
      could duplicate a sequence. Rebinding the same run was rejected because it
      breaks one-stream identity and ordering. Starting a new run remains a host
      choice only when the owning operation permits a distinct invocation; it is
      never recovery for the failed run. Rejecting only an invalid call and
      continuing was rejected because queued calls were accepted against state the
      caller has violated, so hosts could diverge on stage order, terminal
      eligibility, and report reconciliation. Permanent logical closure preserves one
      deterministic failure boundary without writing recovery bytes.

      Revisit when: A transport-neutral transactional framing mechanism exists.
    }

    ProtocolOwnedRunIdentity {
      Decision: Generate runId inside openWriter as a cryptographically random
      canonical lowercase UUID version 4.

      Scope: Governs decentralized event-run correlation; excludes session, proposal,
      report, adapter, and deterministic-replay identities.

      Trade-offs: Callers cannot choose runId and replay receives a fresh identity.

      Discarded alternatives: Caller strings were rejected because uniqueness and
      canonical form vary by host. A global sequence was rejected because it needs a
      shared allocator. Noncryptographic randomness was rejected because independent
      hostile or faulty hosts could make collisions materially easier.

      Consequences: Every successful writer exposes a portable collision-resistant
      identity whose exact representation is stable across hosts.

      Revisit when: A host-neutral identity standard provides equivalent independent
      generation and collision resistance.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationEventWriterProtocol {
      If sequence 3 progress is atomically suppressed after 1 and 2 deliver, later
      progress is validated then suppressed and the terminal uses sequence 3.

      A terminal queued behind protocol-invalid, exhaustion, incompatibility, or
      zero-byte unavailability performs no write and respectively returns
      protocol-invalid, protocol-exhausted, stream-incompatible, or
      terminal-unavailable. Queued nonterminals use stream-unavailable for the last
      case.

      Two overlapping diagnostics write in acceptance order. A call submitted after
      terminal acceptance returns protocol-invalid without writing.

      Started zero-byte failure returns stream-unavailable; partial started returns
      stream-incompatible. No automatic retry occurs.

      A failed or cancelled terminal may follow structurally valid unique diagnostic
      progress without report reconciliation. A completed terminal reconciles all
      delivered progress against its report.
    }
  }
}
